<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  ANDROID MANIFEST OVERLAY.

  Copied over the file Capacitor generates (`npx cap add android`) by
  scripts/package-apk.mjs, which runs AFTER generation and before Gradle. It is an
  overlay rather than a hand-maintained project because the whole android/
  tree is generated: committing it would mean merging upstream template changes
  by hand on every Capacitor upgrade.

  WHY EACH PERMISSION IS NEEDED FOR A 24/7 OVERNIGHT RUN:

    INTERNET              the whole point — Mothership and Master traffic.
    ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE  required before Android grants INTERNET on API 28+.
    WAKE_LOCK             keeps the CPU on with the screen off. Without it the
                           device sleeps between fetches and the node silently
                           stops mid-run around 11pm.
    FOREGROUND_SERVICE    lets the process outlive the UI.
    FOREGROUND_SERVICE_DATA_SYNC
                           Android 14 (API 34) REQUIRES a typed foreground
                           service and rejects a startForeground() call with no
                           matching type. Without this the service throws
                           ForegroundServiceStartNotAllowedException on any
                           modern tablet and the app dies at launch.
    POST_NOTIFICATIONS    Android 13+ requires an explicit grant, or the
                           persistent notification is silently never shown and
                           the service looks dead to the user.
    RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED
                           restarts the service after a reboot, so "overnight"
                           survives someone plugging the tablet in.
    FOREGROUND_SERVICE_SPECIAL_USE
                           lets a service declare WHY it must keep running.
                           NOTE: declared for safety, but NOT used by the service
                           below, which is typed `dataSync` and therefore pairs
                           with FOREGROUND_SERVICE_DATA_SYNC instead. Android
                           requires the permission to MATCH the declared type, so
                           adding it while keeping `dataSync` is harmless whereas
                           the reverse (declaring `specialUse` without the
                           permission) throws at startForeground().
    REQUEST_INSTALL_PACKAGES  lets this app hand a downloaded .apk to the system
                            package installer, which is what the in-app updater in
                            MobileBridge.java does. It is NOT auto-granted on
                            Android 8+: the first update takes the operator to
                            Settings > "Allow from this source", and MobileBridge
                            checks canRequestPackageInstalls() and opens that screen
                            itself rather than failing with an opaque
                            ActivityNotFoundException. Not being auto-granted is
                            FINE, and is in fact the point: a sideloaded node with
                            no Play Store account can still be updated by a human
                            in front of the tablet.
    READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE    needed on API <= 32 to read an .apk that is not inside
                            the app sandbox. maxSdkVersion="32" is deliberate and
                            must stay: from API 33 this permission is REPLACED by
                            the granular READ_MEDIA_* permissions, which do not
                            cover APKs, so asking for it there buys nothing.
                            The updater actually writes into getExternalCacheDir()
                            — app-scoped storage that needs no permission at all —
                            so this declaration is belt-and-braces for OEM builds
                            that hand the installer a shared path instead.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

  <!--
    NO BATTERY_STATS HERE, AND IT MUST NOT COME BACK.

    `android.permission.BATTERY_STATS` was previously declared "for parity with
    the power-awareness feature". It is gone now, and the reason it should stay
    gone is worth writing down, because it reads like an omission otherwise:

      1. It is a signature|privileged permission. A sideloaded APK is NEVER
         granted it, so it did nothing except sit in the manifest.
      2. Declaring a privileged permission you cannot hold is exactly the kind
         of thing that trips a SecurityException on modern Android, which is the
         reported symptom: instant crash at launch, "Something went wrong with
         Rippa Node".
      3. Removing it costs nothing, because the battery reading never came from
         it. ACTION_BATTERY_CHANGED is a broadcast any app may observe with zero
         permissions, which is precisely what @capacitor/device's
         getBatteryInfo() does. Battery reporting is unaffected by this change;
         only a declaration that was never load-bearing is gone.

    Net: fewer permissions, same behaviour, no privileged-permission declaration
    that could fail the boot path.
  -->
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.WAKE_LOCK" />
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" />
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_DATA_SYNC" />
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED" />
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_SPECIAL_USE" />

  <!-- The in-app APK updater. See the header comment for why each one is here. -->
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.REQUEST_INSTALL_PACKAGES" />
  <uses-permission
      android:name="android.permission.READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE"
      android:maxSdkVersion="32" />

  <application
      android:allowBackup="true"
      android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
      android:label="@string/app_name"
      android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
      android:supportsRtl="true"
      android:theme="@style/AppTheme"
      android:usesCleartextTraffic="true">

    <!--
      NO `android:name` ON THE <application> TAG. DO NOT ADD ONE.

      This used to read `android:name="com.rippaadventures.node.RippaNodeApplication"`
      and THAT IS WHY THE APK CRASHED INSTANTLY ON EVERY LAUNCH.

      Android instantiates the Application class in ActivityThread.handleBindApplication,
      BEFORE any Activity is created, before the WebView exists and before the
      package's data directory is populated. Naming a class that is not in the APK
      therefore kills the process at the earliest possible moment, with:

        java.lang.RuntimeException: Unable to create application
          com.rippaadventures.node.RippaNodeApplication: ClassNotFoundException

      No <uses-permission> can prevent or fix that — the app never reaches the point
      where a permission is consulted. The diagnostic signature is exactly what was
      reported: instant crash, zero bytes of Data/Cache, because /data/data/<pkg>
      is never created.

      Nothing needs a custom Application class here. Capacitor's BridgeActivity
      builds the bridge and registers plugins itself (RippaNodePlugin via
      @CapacitorPlugin, the Device plugin via cap sync), so the default
      android.app.Application is correct. `scripts/package-apk.mjs` now fails the
      build if this overlay ever names a component class that is not actually
      staged, so this cannot silently come back.
    -->

    <!--
      THE LAUNCHER ACTIVITY. THE REASON THIS APK HAD NO APP-DRAWER ICON.

      This overlay REPLACES the manifest Capacitor generates (see the note at the
      top of this file and scripts/package-apk.mjs). Capacitor's own template
      declares MainActivity — that is where `npx cap add android` puts the
      MAIN/LAUNCHER intent-filter. Replacing the file wholesale therefore
      DELETED the launcher entry along with everything we intended to override.

      The result is a genuinely confusing failure: the APK installs fine, the
      foreground service runs, the node joins the swarm — and there is no icon
      anywhere. Nothing errors, so it presents as "the build is broken" when in
      fact the build was perfect and the app was simply un-launchable.

      `configChanges` is not optional either. Without it, Android destroys and
      recreates the activity on every rotation or theme change, which tears down
      the WebView and with it every swarm poll timer. Capacitor's template
      declares this list; dropping it reintroduces the same class of bug.

      `android:screenOrientation` is deliberately NOT LOCKED. It was
      `portrait`, on the theory that this is a desk appliance that should never
      rotate. That was wrong for the actual fleet: these tablets are mounted in
      landscape (dashboard screens, counter-top nodes) and being pinned to
      portrait left operators rotating a device that did not respond, or
      installing a build and still seeing the app sideways.

      `unspecified` defers to the device: it honours the user's own auto-rotate
      setting, which is the correct default for hardware we do not control. Note
      the deliberate choice NOT to use `fullSensor`, which ignores the user's
      rotation lock and rotates regardless. That overrides an explicit user
      preference, and is exactly the kind of "app second-guesses the OS"
      behaviour that only bites in the field.

      Rotation stays cheap regardless, because `configChanges` above already
      absorbs `orientation|screenSize`: the activity is NOT recreated on rotate,
      so the WebView and every swarm poll timer survive a rotation intact. That
      is the property that makes unlocking safe, and it was already in place.
    -->

    <!--
      THE FILE PROVIDER — the only way the system package installer may read our
      downloaded .apk.

      WHY A content:// URI AND NOT A file:// ONE. Since Android 7 (Nougat) the
      installer runs in a separate process with a FileUriExposedException waiting
      for any app that hands it a raw file path. FileProvider maps a private cache
      file onto a short-lived, permission-scoped content:// URI, which is what
      makes the install actually work on a modern tablet.

      ${applicationId}.provider is a manifest-merger placeholder, so the authority
      tracks applicationId automatically. MobileBridge.java resolves the very same
      string at runtime with `context.getPackageName() + ".provider"` — those two
      MUST stay in step, because a mismatch does not fail loudly, it throws
      IllegalArgumentException ("Failed to find configured root") the first time an
      operator tries to update. scripts/package-apk.mjs asserts both halves at
      build time so they cannot drift apart silently.

      @xml/file_paths is staged from mobile/android-overlay/res/xml/file_paths.xml.
      Capacitor's template does ship a file_paths.xml, but it maps `external-path`
      (the whole shared volume) and not `external-cache-path`, so without the
      overlay the provider would throw for the file we actually download to.
    -->
    <provider
        android:name="androidx.core.content.FileProvider"
        android:authorities="${applicationId}.provider"
        android:exported="false"
        android:grantUriPermissions="true">
      <meta-data
          android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
          android:resource="@xml/file_paths" />
    </provider>
    <activity
        android:name=".MainActivity"
        android:exported="true"
        android:launchMode="singleTask"
        android:screenOrientation="unspecified"
        android:theme="@style/AppTheme.NoActionBarLaunch"
        android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|locale|smallestScreenSize|screenLayout|uiMode|navigation|density|fontScale">
      <!--
        THE LAUNCHER INTENT FILTER — verbatim what Capacitor's template emits.
        Without BOTH lines the app is installed but un-launchable; with only
        MAIN and no LAUNCHER category it is equally invisible in the drawer.
      -->
      <intent-filter>
        <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
        <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
      </intent-filter>
    </activity>

    <!--
      THE NODE'S OWN FOREGROUND SERVICE.

      exported=false: nothing outside this app may start it, because starting it
      pins a wake lock and shows a permanent notification.

      stopWithTask=false: swiping the app away must NOT take the node with it.
      That is the entire difference between an overnight satellite and an app
      that dies the first time someone tidies up their tablet.

      foregroundServiceType="dataSync" matches the FOREGROUND_SERVICE_DATA_SYNC
      permission declared above — the two must agree on API 34+ or the platform
      throws at startForeground().
    -->
    <service
        android:name=".RippaNodeService"
        android:enabled="true"
        android:exported="false"
        android:stopWithTask="false"
        android:foregroundServiceType="dataSync">
      <!--
        Required on API 34+: the platform audits every long-running foreground
        service against a declared reason. Without this Play Store review (and
        some OEM builds) rejects the start outright.
      -->
      <property
          android:name="android.app.PROPERTY_SPECIAL_USE_FGS_SUBTYPE"
          android:value="Autonomous swarm node: polls the Rippa Mothership on a
                           timer and must survive the screen turning off to keep
                          foraging for the fleet." />
    </service>

    <!--
      BOOT RECEIVER: brings the service back after a reboot or an app update.

      The node is expected to survive "someone left the tablet on the desk", and
      a tablet that reboots for an update at 3am would otherwise silently drop
      out of the swarm until noticed the next morning.
    -->
    <receiver
        android:name=".RippaNodeBootReceiver"
        android:enabled="true"
        android:exported="true">
      <intent-filter>
        <action android:name="android.intent.action.BOOT_COMPLETED" />
        <action android:name="android.intent.action.MY_PACKAGE_REPLACED" />
      </intent-filter>
    </receiver>
  </application>
</manifest>